    "answer": {
      "short": "$y=2ax-a^2$.\n\nA single intersection requires discriminant $(m-2a)^2=0$, giving $m=2a$.",
      "worked": "Write the line through $(a,a^2)$ as $y-a^2=m(x-a)$. Substituting $y=x^2$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-a^2&=m(x-a)\\\\x^2-mx+ma-a^2&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThere is exactly one intersection, so the discriminant is zero:\n$$\\begin{align*}0&=m^2-4(ma-a^2)\\\\0&=m^2-4ma+4a^2\\\\0&=(m-2a)^2\\\\m&=2a.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}y-a^2&=2a(x-a)\\\\y&=2ax-a^2,\\end{align*}$$\nas required.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
